---
import Page from '../../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { DEMO_URL, DOCKER_HUB_URL } from '../../../site';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Which identity providers work with QuizDock?',
    a: '<p>Any OpenID Connect provider, in principle: QuizDock uses Discovery, the authorization code flow with PKCE and the provider’s signing keys, nothing provider-specific. Keycloak is the one tested and shipped, in the full setup; others have not been tested. See <a href="/docs/operator/oidc/">OIDC</a>.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I see all the results of one trainee across several trainings?',
    a: '<p>Not on one page. Results are recorded under the trainee’s account in each session, and the history is kept per quiz. A room’s standings add up the quizzes played in it. To follow a trainee across sessions, combine the CSV exports. See <a href="/docs/host/reports-and-history/">Reports and history</a>.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does QuizDock connect to an LMS?',
    a: '<p>No. QuizDock has no LMS connector; results leave as CSV files (semicolon-separated, UTF-8) per session, per room and per player. See <a href="/docs/host/export-results/">Export results</a>.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can external guests join a session without an account?',
    a: '<p>Yes, if the operator allows open access. The trainer then chooses <b>Open access</b> at launch: the PIN and a nickname are enough, and no individual result is kept for that session. See <a href="/docs/host/start-a-room/">Start a room</a>.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long are training results kept?',
    a: '<p>Archived sessions are kept 365 days, then deleted when the operator runs the purge command; nothing deletes them automatically, and the period is not a setting. See <a href="/docs/admin/privacy-and-retention/">Privacy and retention</a>.</p>',
  },
];
---

<Page
  title="QuizDock for enterprise training"
  description="Training quizzes with single sign-on: results kept per trainee and per session, CSV exports, standings across a training day, templates shared between trainers."
  faq={faq}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <h1>Training quizzes whose results you keep, per trainee</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      After a module you want to know what stuck, for the group and for each trainee, and to keep a record of
      it. With QuizDock connected to your identity provider, trainees sign in with their work account, results
      are archived under that account, and every session exports to CSV.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How do I know who is who without a sign-up sheet?</h2>
      <p>
        In OIDC mode, trainees sign in with the account they already have, through your OpenID Connect
        provider; the tokens stay on the server, never in the browser. Each phone tells its trainee “Your taking
        part and your score are recorded under your account.”
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Let participants pick their display name</b> decides what the room sees: off, the name comes from
        the account; on, trainees choose one, and their results stay attached to the account all the same. See
        <a href="/docs/operator/oidc/">OIDC</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Can I decide, session by session, what is recorded?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. When the operator allows open access, <b>Present</b> asks for the <b>Participant access</b>:
        <b>Accounts required</b> or <b>Open access</b>, with <b>Remember my choice</b> to stop asking. In the
        lobby, <b>Before starting</b> holds the rest, fixed once the quiz starts:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Personalised tracking</b>, on by default: results under each trainee’s account; off, only the group’s overall results.</li>
        <li><b>Record all answers</b>, off by default: every answer of every trainee, after a confirmation that warns it is personal data.</li>
        <li><b>Who hears the sound in this quiz</b>, when the quiz has sound or video.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Trainees see a notice that matches these choices. See <a href="/docs/host/start-a-room/">Start a room</a>
        and <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where are a session’s results afterwards?</h2>
      <p>
        In the quiz’s <b>History</b>: each archived session with its date, status, participants and success
        rate. A session shows <b>Results by question</b> (answers, success rate, average time) and the
        <b>Players</b> table (rank, nickname, score, correct answers, streak). <b>Export (CSV)</b> downloads
        that table.
      </p>
      <p>
        A trainee’s page lists their answers question by question, with its own <b>Export (CSV)</b>, when
        <b>Record all answers</b> was on. There is no page that gathers one trainee across quizzes, and no LMS
        connector: the CSV files are the way out. See <a href="/docs/host/reports-and-history/">Reports and
        history</a> and <a href="/docs/host/export-results/">Export results</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>A training day: several quizzes, one ranking</h2>
      <p>
        One room can hold the whole day. After a quiz, <b>Next quiz</b> picks the following one: trainees stay
        in the room and nobody types the PIN again. The picker marks what was <b>Already played</b> and
        <b>Not played yet</b>. <b>End quiz…</b> stops one midway, with <b>Keep what was played so far</b>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each quiz scores from zero; between quizzes the projection shows the <b>Room standings</b>, and the day
        ends on the <b>Room podium</b>. In History, the room’s standings add up its archived quizzes and leave
        with <b>Export standings (CSV)</b>, provided every quiz of the room had <b>Personalised tracking</b> on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How do trainers share their material?</h2>
      <p>
        Two ways. <b>Share with the instance’s other hosts</b> puts a quiz in their list, read-only, and they
        create their own copy from it. <b>Share as a template</b> puts a copy of a ready quiz, with its licence,
        in <b>Shared templates</b>, where any trainer chooses <b>Create a quiz from this</b>; sharing again
        replaces the entry, and <b>Withdraw</b> removes it without touching the copies taken.
      </p>
      <p>
        Between instances, <b>Export</b> writes a <code>.quizdock.zip</code> with the media, which
        <b>Import</b> turns into a new draft. See <a href="/docs/host/templates-and-community/">Templates and
        community</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>And the administration?</h2>
      <p>
        Roles are Participant, Host and Manager, read from your provider’s roles claim or granted in the
        administration’s <b>Accounts</b> page; a manager administers without presenting. When a trainer leaves,
        an administrator transfers their quizzes to another account, media and history included. The operator
        can lock settings, and every administrative change, from the web or the command line, is written to an
        audit trail that is never edited. See <a href="/enterprise/">QuizDock for organisations</a>,
        <a href="/docs/admin/accounts-and-roles/">Accounts and roles</a> and <a href="/docs/admin/audit/">Audit</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="next">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={DEMO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try the live demo ↗</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={DOCKER_HUB_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get it on Docker Hub ↗</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/host/getting-started/">Getting started as a host</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
